/*!
   \qmlmodule org.kde.kirigamiaddons.settings
   \title Kirigami Addons Settings QML Types
   \brief Pages and navigation for application settings.

   The Settings module presents an application's configuration pages in a
   desktop window or in the application's page stack on mobile. Import it
   with an alias:

   \qml
   import org.kde.kirigamiaddons.settings as Settings
   \endqml

   \section1 Organizing settings pages

   A \l ConfigurationView owns a list of \l ConfigurationModule objects.
   Each module describes one settings page: its identifier, label, icon, and
   the component used to create the page. The view shows a list beside the
   selected page on desktop. On mobile, it shows the list first and opens a
   selected page in the application window's page stack.

   A desktop settings window with the selected page beside the module list:

   \image settingsdialogdesktop.png Desktop settings window with the Appearance page selected

   A mobile settings page with modules grouped under headings:

   \image settingsdialogmobile.png Mobile settings page with Settings and About groups

   Set \c window to the application's \c Kirigami.ApplicationWindow
   before calling \c open(). A module's \c page property is a function that
   returns a \c Component. A \l {FormCardPage} is a convenient root for a
   settings page:

   \qml
   import QtQuick
   import QtQuick.Controls as Controls
   import org.kde.kirigami as Kirigami
   import org.kde.kirigamiaddons.formcard as FormCard
   import org.kde.kirigamiaddons.settings as Settings

   Kirigami.ApplicationWindow {
       id: window

       Component {
           id: generalPage

           FormCard.FormCardPage {
               title: i18nc("@title", "General")

               FormCard.FormHeader {
                   title: i18nc("@title:group", "Appearance")
               }

               FormCard.FormCard {
                   FormCard.FormSwitchDelegate {
                       text: i18nc("@option:check", "Use dark colors")
                   }
               }
           }
       }

       Settings.ConfigurationView {
           id: settingsView
           window: window

           modules: [
               Settings.ConfigurationModule {
                   moduleId: "general"
                   text: i18nc("@action:button", "General")
                   icon.name: "preferences-system-symbolic"
                   page: () => generalPage
               }
           ]
       }

       Controls.Button {
           text: i18nc("@action:button", "Settings")
           onClicked: settingsView.open()
       }
   }
   \endqml

   Add more \l ConfigurationModule objects to \c modules for more pages.
   Give each module a distinct \c moduleId. Pass that ID to
   \c {settingsView.open("general")} to select a page when opening the
   settings view. Without an ID, desktop opens the first module and mobile
   shows the module list.

   \section1 Controlling modules

   Set a module's \c visible property to hide it from the settings list.
   Set \c category to a translated heading to group modules on mobile;
   modules with the default category appear in the main Settings group.

   The view creates each page on its first visit and caches it for later
   visits. Use \c initialProperties to pass values needed at creation time.
   For example, if the page component declares a \c {required property string
   accountName}, initialize it like this:

   \qml
   Settings.ConfigurationModule {
       moduleId: "account"
       text: i18nc("@action:button", "Account")
       page: () => accountPage
       initialProperties: () => ({ accountName: window.activeAccountName })
   }
   \endqml

   Changes to these values after the page has been created need regular QML
   bindings or signals; \c initialProperties is called only when creating
   the page.

   \section1 Ready-made modules

   \l SpellcheckingConfigurationModule supplies a spellchecking settings
   page when the desktop style supports it. \l ShortcutsConfigurationModule
   supplies a keyboard shortcut editor on desktop and requires an
   \l {AbstractKirigamiApplication} from the Actions module through its
   \c application property. Add either type to \c modules alongside your
   own \l ConfigurationModule objects.

   \l CategorizedSettings and \l SettingAction are deprecated. Use
   \l ConfigurationView and \l ConfigurationModule for new settings pages.

   \sa ConfigurationView
   \sa ConfigurationModule
   \sa SpellcheckingConfigurationModule
   \sa ShortcutsConfigurationModule
 */
